   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to repeal paragraph (h) of subdivision 12-G of section 210 and paragraph 8 of subsection (nn) of section 606 of the tax law, relating to the qualified emerging technology company facilities, operations and training credit",
    "basePrintNo": "S5633",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5633-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Rpld §210 sub 12-G ¶ (h), §606 sub§ (nn) ¶ 8, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5633\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto repeal paragraph (h) of subdivision 12-G of section 210 and paragraph\n8 of subsection (nn) of section 606 of the tax law, relating to the\nqualified emerging technology company facilities, operations and training\ncredit\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nThis legislation makes permanent the\nqualified emerging technology company facilities, operations and\nnaming credit.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nThe Qualified Emerging Technology\nCompany Facilities, Operations and Training Credit, set out in New\nYork State Tax Law sections 210 (12-G) and 606 (nn) are set to expire\nDecember 31, 2011. This legislation makes permanent these sections of\ntax law by repealing paragraph (h) of subdivision 12-G of section\n210, and paragraph S of subsection (nn) of section 606, of the New\nYork State Tax Law.\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nThese parts of New York State Tax Law\ndeal with three\ntax credits for small companies and individuals engaged in\nhigh-technology fields: a research and development property credit, a\nqualified research expenses credit, and a qualified high technology\ntraining credit.\n\nThis bill makes permanent theses parts of Tax Law by repealing\nspecific provisions that allow the credits to expire on December 31,\n2031.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNone.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nEstimated tax expenditures for this tax credit\nwere 515 million in 2007.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
