    "lawCode": "Rpld §210 sub 12-G ¶ (h), §606 sub§ (nn) ¶ 8, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5633A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto repeal paragraph (h) of subdivision 12-G of section 210 and paragraph\n8 of subsection (nn) of section 606 of the tax law, relating to the\nqualified emerging technology company facilities, operations and training\ncredit;\nand providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nThis legislation makes permanent the qualified emerging technology\ncompany facilities, operations and naming credit.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nThe Qualified Emerging Technology Company Facilities, Operations and\nTraining Credit, set out in New York State Tax Law sections 210\n(12-G) and 606 (nn) are set to expire December 31, 2011. This\nlegislation makes permanent these sections of tax law by repealing\nparagraph (h) of subdivision 12-G of section 210, and paragraph S of\nsubsection (nn) of section 606, of the New York State Tax Law.\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nThese parts of New York State Tax Law deal with three tax credits for\nsmall companies and individuals engaged in high-technology fields: a\nresearch and development property credit, a qualified research\nexpenses credit, and a qualified high technology training credit.\n\nThis bill makes permanent theses parts of Tax Law by repealing\nspecific provisions that allow the credits to expire on December 31,\n2031.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNone.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nEstimated tax expenditures for this tax credit were 515 million in 2007.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
